" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Fang I-chih\">Fang I-chih</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fan_Wencheng\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Fan Wencheng\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367170\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367170\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3637594</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Fan W\u00ean-ch&#39;\u00eang</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Fang Chao-ying</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"231\" data-page-number=\"231\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/243\" data-page-index=\"243\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/243\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;243\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>FAN</b> W\u00ean-ch'\u00eang \u8303\u6587\u7a0b (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u61b2\u6597,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u7147\u5dbd), 1597\u20131666, Aug. 31, one of the first four Grand Secretaries of the Ch'ing dynasty, a native of Fu-shun, near Sh\u00ean-yang (Mukden), was a great-grandson of Fan Ts'ung \u8303\u93d3 (<i>chin-shih</i> of 1517) who was a president of the Board of War in the Ming period. When <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Nurhaci\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Nurhaci\">Nurhaci</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] took Fu-shun in 1618 (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Yung-fang\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Yung-fang\">Li Yung-fang</a></span>), Fan W\u00ean-ch'\u00eang surrendered to the Manchus and was shown much favor. When in 1629 Manchu troops under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Abahai\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Abahai\">Abahai</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] pillaged their way to the walls of Peking and suffered a defeat at the hands of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Y%C3%BCan_Ch%27ung-huan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Y\u00fcan Ch&#39;ung-huan\">Y\u00fcan Ch'ung-huan</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], Fan invented the story that Y\u00fcan was in league with the Manchus. The Ming emperor became suspicious, arrested and finally executed Y\u00fcan. Fan, in turn, was rewarded by Abahai with a minor hereditary title. Abahai relied much on him in the founding of the central government at Mukden, making him in 1636 a Grand Secretary\u2014his colleagues being Ganglin (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dorgon\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dorgon\">Dorgon</a></span>), Hife (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Songgotu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Songgotu\">Songgotu</a></span>), and Pao Ch'\u00eang-hsien \u9b91\u627f\u5148. Involved in 1643 in a conspiracy with Adali and \u0160oto (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Lekedehun\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Lekedehun\">Lekedehun</a></span> and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dorgon\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dorgon\">Dorgon</a></span>), Fan and his family, then belonging to the Plain Red Banner (?), were transferred to the Chinese Bordered Yellow Banner. When the news of the fall of Peking to <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Tz%C5%AD-ch%27%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch&#39;\u00eang\">Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] reached&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"232\" data-page-number=\"232\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/244\" data-page-index=\"244\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/244\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;244\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>Mukden in 1644 Fan memorialized the princes regent urging them to seize this opportunity to conquer an empire, but in so doing to spare the lives of the common people and refrain from the destruction that characterized former invasions.\n</p><p>Fan W\u00ean-Ch'\u00eang accompanied <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dorgon\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dorgon\">Dorgon</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] in the expedition to Peking and suggested to him many ways of putting into effect the new Manchu regime and adapting it to the Chinese political organization. He recommended, for example, the introduction of extra sessions of the civil-service examinations, remittance of burdensome taxes, and the proper burial of the last Ming emperor. Although partially involved in 1651 in the case of Ganglin and Kicungge (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dorgon\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dorgon\">Dorgon</a></span>) who were condemned to death for having altered the official records of the reign of Nurhaci, Fan got off with but a brief suspension from office. In the following year he was made a member of the Council of princes and high officials and was raised to the hereditary rank of viscount of the first class. He retired in 1654, and three years later the emperor ordered that his portrait be painted and kept in the palace. He was canonized as W\u00ean-su \u6587\u8085. Of his sons the best known was <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Fan_Ch%27%C3%AAng-mo\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Fan Ch&#39;\u00eang-mo\">Fan Ch'\u00eang-mo</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>].\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/238/2b; 3/1/3a; 4/4/1a; <i>Tung-hua lu</i>, Shun-chih 1/4b, 2/4b; <i>China Review</i> IX, 1880-81, pp. 95\u201397; Fan Ch'\u00eang-mo, <i>Fan Chung-Ch'\u00ean kung w\u00ean-chi</i>, 5/2b.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Fang Chao-ying</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Abahai"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Dorgon"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Fan Ch'\u00eang-mo"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Fan Mou-chu"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Fang I-chih"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Lekedehun"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Yung-fang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Nurhaci"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Songgotu"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Y\u00fcan Ch'ung-huan"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Fang Chao-ying"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = fan wen-ch'eng\n | previous = Fan Mou-chu\n | next     = Fang I-chih\n | from     = 243\n | to       = 244\n | notes    = \n | section_author = Fang Chao-ying\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Fan W\u00ean-ch'\u00eang}}"}}}